# VLAN Segmentation Lab

## Objective
Demonstrate how VLANs work and configure basic VLAN segmentation on a single switch.

## Setup
1x Cisco 2960T-24 switch
4 PCs (PC0–PC3)
VLAN 2 and VLAN 3 created on the switch, alongside the default VLAN

## What I did
1. Built a topology connecting 4 PCs to the switch
2. Created VLAN 2 and VLAN 3 in the VLAN database
3. Assigned ports to their respective VLANs
4. Verified connectivity using the `ping` command

## Port assignments

| Port | VLAN |
|------|------|
| FastEthernet0/1 | 2 |
| FastEthernet0/2 | 2 |
| FastEthernet0/3 | 3 |
| FastEthernet0/4 | 3 |

Ports were set to Access mode and manually assigned to their VLAN.

## Verification

**Same VLAN ping (PC0 → 10.0.0.2):**
0% packet loss, average RTT ~8ms

**Cross-VLAN ping (PC0 → 10.0.0.3):**
100% packet loss — request timed out

## Takeaway
VLANs segment broadcast domains at Layer 2. Hosts in different VLANs can't communicate without a Layer 3 device (router or L3 switch) to route between them. This confirms VLAN isolation is working as configured.
